    "To understand the engineering necessity of high-resolution monotonic clocks, one must study the synchronization protocols that govern modern cloud server clusters. In multi-tenant cloud environments like AWS, Google Cloud, and Azure, physical server motherboards experience minute crystal oscillator drift caused by ambient temperature changes in server racks. To keep clocks synchronized, operating system kernels continuously run daemon processes like chrony or systemd-timesyncd. These daemons synchronize with atomic clock stratum-1 servers via Network Time Protocol. When a drift of twenty milliseconds is detected, older systems would perform a step adjustment: instantly resetting the system wall clock backward. If an application calculates elapsed duration by evaluating `const duration = Date.now() - start`, and a clock step adjustment occurs between those two lines, the calculated duration will be negative, corrupting telemetry averages and triggering arithmetic underflow bugs.",
